# ============================================================
# TAILORING PRINCIPLES
# ============================================================

tailoring_principles:

  - >
    Analyze the JD semantically rather than performing
    simple keyword matching.

  - >
    Extract core responsibilities, required skills,
    preferred skills, tools, domain concepts,
    business problems, and seniority expectations.

  - >
    Classify important JD requirements as DIRECT,
    TRANSFERABLE, or UNSUPPORTED.

  - >
    Use DIRECT evidence strongly.

  - >
    Use TRANSFERABLE evidence carefully without claiming
    exact experience that is not supported.

  - >
    Never use UNSUPPORTED technologies or responsibilities
    as if they were candidate experience.

  - >
    Use JD terminology when it accurately describes
    an existing candidate capability.

  - >
    Prioritize factual relevance over keyword density.

  - >
    Do not repeat keywords excessively.

  - >
    Prefer quantified achievements when relevant.

  - >
    Use the strongest 4–6 experience bullets per role.

  - >
    Select 2–3 projects based on JD relevance.

  - >
    Never rename a project.

  - >
    Never attach one project's achievement to another project.

  - >
    Preserve official employment titles.

  - >
    Preserve employment dates.

  - >
    Preserve education.

  - >
    Preserve certifications.

  - >
    Use ATS-friendly language.

  - >
    Keep the summary concise.

  - >
    Make the resume demonstrate capability rather than
    simply listing keywords.

  - >
    If the JD asks for a tool that is not present,
    do not fabricate it.

  - >
    If a JD requirement is adjacent to an existing capability,
    use transferable language rather than claiming direct experience.


